{% doc %}
  One product in the bundle picker, as a wide row — the product tile the drawing
  calls for, and the slide `snippets/carousel-shell.liquid` pages through.

  ## One file, two states

  `placeholder: true` renders the same row with nothing behind it, for a
  category block whose collection has not been chosen yet.

  It is the same file rather than a second `bundle-row-placeholder.liquid`
  because there is no reason for it not to be: the row is a `for` loop's output,
  not a tree of theme blocks, so nothing stops the placeholder walking the same
  markup. `snippets/article-card-placeholder.liquid` is a separate file only
  because its real card is built from blocks and `{% content_for 'blocks' %}`
  takes no parameters — it had no choice, and its own comment says so.

  Every class name, every wrapper and every gap is therefore literally the same
  in both states, and cannot drift, because there is only one of each.

  What the placeholder does not have is data: no `data-product-id`, no
  `data-variant-id`, no price the customer could act on. So its add button is
  `disabled` and its title is not a link. An onboarding card that adds a product
  that does not exist is worse than one that plainly does nothing.

  ## The saving badge is `snippets/badge.liquid`

  Not a percentage computed here. The badge component already knows the rules —
  sold out beats preorder beats sale, the merchant's choice of percentage,
  amount or plain text, and the custom tag badges — and it shows a saving only
  when the variant genuinely has a `compare_at_price` above its price, which is
  a discount configured in Shopify admin and nothing else.

  This row used to do its own `compare_at_price | minus: price | times: 100`,
  which meant the picker could disagree with the same product's card three
  sections up the page: a different label, a different threshold, and a number
  that kept being calculated after a merchant had set the badge to show an
  amount instead.

  ## The quantity stepper

  `snippets/quantity-selector.liquid` at its compact size, at the start of the
  foot row — exactly where the size line used to be. It replaces it: "850g",
  from a metafield, is what the product page and the grid card are for, and a
  row in a bundle picker is a thing being *taken*, so how many is the question
  the customer actually has on it.

  `<product-bundle>` reads the input when the row is added, so the stepper is a
  real number input the component looks up rather than a display of one.

  @param {product} [product] - Omit with `placeholder: true`.
  @param {boolean} [placeholder] - Draw the empty state.
  @param {number} [index] - Position in the list. Picks the placeholder picture.
  @param {boolean} [show_rating] - Default from `settings.show_rating`.
  @param {boolean} [show_quantity] - Default true.
  @param {number} [max_per_product] - The stepper's maximum.
  @param {string} [id_prefix] - Namespaces the quantity input's id. Required when two categories can render the same product.

  @example
    {% render 'bundle-row', product: product, index: forloop.index0, id_prefix: panel_id %}

  @example
    {% render 'bundle-row', placeholder: true, index: i, id_prefix: panel_id %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign is_placeholder = false
  if placeholder or product == blank
    assign is_placeholder = true
  endif

  assign position = index | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign prefix = id_prefix | default: 'bundle'

  assign with_rating = show_rating
  if with_rating == nil
    assign with_rating = section.settings.show_rating
  endif

  assign with_quantity = show_quantity
  if with_quantity == nil
    assign with_quantity = true
  endif

  assign quantity_max = max_per_product | default: section.settings.max_per_product | default: 4 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the card's own colour scheme ----

    A card is a surface inside a surface — a pale tile on a dark section, or the
    reverse — and until now the only way to say so was **Card background**, one
    colour that changed the tile and nothing on it. The title stayed the
    section's text colour, the prices stayed its heading colour and the add
    button stayed its outline, so a dark card was a dark card with black type on
    it.

    The scheme is the theme's own answer to that, and it is the Group block's
    idiom exactly: a checkbox that means "the one around me", and a picker that
    is only asked about when the checkbox is off. `snippets/group.liquid` writes
    the same two classes from the same pair of settings, so a merchant who has
    coloured a Group already knows this control.

    `color-scheme-container` is what makes it work on the card rather than only
    under it: the class redefines every `--color-*` token *on this element*, so
    everything inside resolves against the card's scheme instead of the
    section's — including `--color-secondary-background`, which is what the card
    is painted with when no explicit background is set.

    Read from `section.settings` rather than passed in, for the reason the row
    count and the rating switch already are: it is one decision about the whole
    picker, and threading it through two call sites in
    `blocks/_product-bundle-category.liquid` would be the same value written
    twice.

    Inherit is the default, so every card in every store that already has this
    section is painted exactly as it was.
  endcomment
  assign card_scheme_classes = ''
  if section.settings.card_inherit_color_scheme == false and section.settings.card_color_scheme != blank
    assign card_scheme_classes = ' color-scheme-container color-' | append: section.settings.card_color_scheme
  endif
-%}

{%- if is_placeholder -%}
  {%- liquid
    comment
      Shopify ships `product-apparel-1` through `-3`. Cycling them means three
      placeholder rows are three different photographs rather than the same one
      repeated, which is what makes the empty state read as "your products go
      here" instead of as a bug. A fourth row starts the cycle again, which is
      the same compromise `article-card-placeholder` makes and for the same
      reason: a repeated photograph is better than a row with no picture.
    endcomment
    assign art = position | modulo: 3 | plus: 1
    assign placeholder_image = 'product-apparel-' | append: art
    assign qty_id = prefix | append: '-placeholder-' | append: position | append: '-qty'
  -%}

  <li class="bundle-row carousel-shell__item{{ card_scheme_classes }}" data-bundle-row-placeholder>
    <div class="bundle-row__media">
      {{ placeholder_image | placeholder_svg_tag: 'bundle-row__image placeholder-svg' }}
    </div>

    <div class="bundle-row__body">
      <div class="bundle-row__head">
        <h3 class="bundle-row__title">{{ 'products.bundle.placeholder_title' | t }}</h3>

        {%- comment -%}
          The badge is drawn rather than rendered, because `badge.liquid` reads a
          real variant and there is not one. It is the same element and the same
          class, so it inherits the real badge's shape, colour and position — the
          empty state shows the merchant where a saving will appear, which is
          most of what the empty state is for.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="badges badges--{{ settings.badge_position }} bundle-row__badges">
          <span class="badge badge--sale">{{ 'products.bundle.placeholder_badge' | t }}</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      {%- if with_rating -%}
        <div class="bundle-row__aside">
          {% render 'rating', value: 4.5, count: 24, class: 'bundle-row__rating' %}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      <div class="bundle-row__foot">
        {%- if with_quantity -%}
          {% render 'quantity-selector',
             id: qty_id,
             min: 1,
             step: 1,
             max: quantity_max,
             size: 'compact',
             class: 'bundle-row__quantity',
             attributes: 'data-bundle-quantity' %}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The same two elements `snippets/price.liquid` emits for a product on
          sale, in the same order, so the placeholder inherits the card's price
          rules — including the one that puts the struck compare-at price first.
          A `<s>` rather than a span with a line through it, because that is
          what the real one is.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="bundle-row__price">
          <span class="price">
            <span class="price__sale">{{ 2500 | money }}</span>
            <s class="price__compare">{{ 3200 | money }}</s>
          </span>
        </span>

        <button
          type="button"
          class="bundle-row__add"
          aria-pressed="false"
          disabled
          aria-label="{{ 'products.bundle.add' | t }}"
        >
          <span class="bundle-row__add-plus">{% render 'icon', icon: 'plus', size: 16 %}</span>
          <span class="bundle-row__add-check">{% render 'icon', icon: 'check', size: 16 %}</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </li>
{%- else -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant

    comment
      A product with real options cannot be dropped into a slot from the row —
      picking one silently is how a customer ends up with a flavour they did not
      choose. Those open the quick add modal instead, and the modal reports the
      chosen variant back to the tray.
    endcomment
    assign needs_options = false
    if product.has_only_default_variant == false and product.variants.size > 1
      assign needs_options = true
    endif

    assign qty_id = prefix | append: '-' | append: product.id | append: '-qty'
  -%}

  <li
    class="bundle-row carousel-shell__item{{ card_scheme_classes }}"
    data-bundle-row
    data-product-id="{{ product.id }}"
    data-variant-id="{{ variant.id }}"
    data-product-url="{{ product.url }}"
    data-needs-options="{{ needs_options }}"
    data-price="{{ variant.price }}"
    data-compare-at-price="{{ variant.compare_at_price | default: 0 }}"
    data-title="{{ product.title | escape }}"
    data-image="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 160 }}"
    {% unless variant.available %}data-unavailable{% endunless %}
  >
    <div class="bundle-row__media">
      {% render 'image',
         image: product.featured_image,
         alt: '',
         ratio: 'square',
         sizes: '(min-width: 750px) 120px, 96px',
         class: 'bundle-row__image' %}
    </div>

    <div class="bundle-row__body">
      <div class="bundle-row__head">
        <h3 class="bundle-row__title">
          <a class="bundle-row__link" href="{{ product.url }}">{{ product.title | escape }}</a>
        </h3>

        {% render 'badge', product: product, variant: variant, class: 'bundle-row__badges' %}
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        The rating's own line, and only the rating on it. The stepper used to
        share it; `Bundle-Porduct-Card.svg` puts the stepper below the rule, at
        the start of the foot — where the size line used to be — with the price
        and the add control at the other end.

        Wrapped in the `if` rather than left as an empty row, because the card
        is a fixed height: an empty 17px line between the title and the rule
        would be 17px the foot no longer has.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if with_rating -%}
        <div class="bundle-row__aside">
          {% render 'rating', product: product, class: 'bundle-row__rating' %}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      <div class="bundle-row__foot">
        {%- if with_quantity -%}
          {% render 'quantity-selector',
             id: qty_id,
             min: 1,
             step: 1,
             max: quantity_max,
             size: 'compact',
             class: 'bundle-row__quantity',
             product_title: product.title,
             attributes: 'data-bundle-quantity' %}
        {%- endif -%}

        <span class="bundle-row__price">
          {% render 'price', variant: variant, context: 'card' %}
        </span>

        {%- comment -%}
          One control, three states, and the state is written by
          `assets/product-bundle.js` rather than by Liquid: a row is only "in the
          bundle" relative to a tray that exists on the client.

          It is a real `<button>` with `aria-pressed`, so a screen reader is told
          the row is a toggle and told which way it is currently set — which is
          the whole of the "a product already in a slot cannot be added again"
          rule, said out loud.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <button
          type="button"
          class="bundle-row__add"
          data-bundle-add
          aria-pressed="false"
          {% unless variant.available %}disabled{% endunless %}
          aria-label="{{ 'products.bundle.add' | t }}: {{ product.title | escape }}"
        >
          <span class="bundle-row__add-plus">{% render 'icon', icon: 'plus', size: 16 %}</span>
          <span class="bundle-row__add-check">{% render 'icon', icon: 'check', size: 16 %}</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </li>
{%- endif -%}
